.TH gummiband 1

.SH NAME
gummiband \- Simple launcher for the X Window System

.SH SYNTAX
\fBgummiband

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fIGummiband\fP is a program launcher for the X Window System. It can be
configured to show machine status (eg battery %, or the time), and to provide
launch buttons (eg a button to run xterm). It provides simple drop-down menus
(eg a button can show the current wifi access point, and clicking on the button
opens a menu allowing a different access point to be selected). An item can
also choose its own colours and offer a tooltip: see \fBTHE NAME PROTOCOL\fP.
.PP
All dynamic content should be provided by shell scripts, which Gummiband will
run according to its configuration.
.PP
Gummiband is based loosely on the 'menu' program by Elliott Hughes.

.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration file (\fI.gummiband\fP) consists of a sequence of key=value
pairs, plus arbitrary ignored blank lines and comments. Comments are denoted
by a '#' character at the start of the line.
.PP
Each item on the gummiband begins with a 'name=' line, after which all further
key=value pairs modify that item, until the next 'name=' identifier. The value
of the 'name' can either be a fixed string, or a program whose output should be
used.
.PP
The following are the valid key types:
.TP 12
.B name
The name to display inside the button, or if the value starts with 'exec ', the
program to run, whose output should be shown in the button. Such a program may
print more than one line: see \fBTHE NAME PROTOCOL\fP.

.TP 12
.B updatesecs
How many seconds between updates of the name. Only to be used when the name=
begins with 'exec '. Value must be at least 1. If not present, or set to a
negative number, the name will be evaluated only once.

.TP 12
.B click
The program to run when the user clicks on the button. Should start with
\&'exec '. Cannot be used in a menu.

.TP 12
.B position
If set to \fIright\fP, the item will be added to the right-hand side of the
menu; if set to \fIleft\fP, or left unset, the item will be added to the left.
Items added to the left are added in left-to-right order; those added to the
right area added right-to-left.

.TP 12
.B menuitems
Comma-separated list of items to propagate in the drop-down menu available from
this item, or, if the value starts with 'exec ', the name of the program whose
output should be used to define the drop-down content. If a program is used,
it will be executed when the drop-down menu is opened.

.TP 12
.B menuclick
Program to run when the user clicks on an item in the drop-down menu. This
string must include the text \fI<item>\fP, which will be substituted with the
string clicked on. Note that if menu items may contain spaces, you should
include quotes around \fI<item>\fP.

.SH "THE NAME PROTOCOL"
A program run for a \fIname=\fP may print more than the line the item is drawn
from. The lines are:
.TP 5
1.
The text to display.
.TP 5
2.
The colours to display it in: one or two colour specifications of the form
\fI#rrggbb\fP, separated by whitespace. The first is the text foreground and
the second the background, so '#ff0000 #0000ff' is red text on blue. A blank
line means the panel's usual colours.
.TP 5
3+.
The text of a tooltip, which appears in a small window below the item while
the pointer rests on it. One line of the tooltip per line of output.
.PP
Any line after the first may be left out, so a program that prints one line
behaves exactly as it always did. The shorter forms of X colour specification
(\fI#rgb\fP, \fI#rrrgggbbb\fP, \fI#rrrrggggbbbb\fP) work as well.
.PP
An item chooses its colours to be noticed, so hovering over it does not take
them away: the highlight replaces the item's background, but the text keeps
the colour the program asked for. The tooltip is drawn in the panel's own
colours whatever the item chose.
.PP
Only \fIname=\fP reads its command's output this way. \fImenuitems=\fP takes
one drop-down entry per line, as it always has.
.PP
A battery script printing
.nf

    17%
    #ffffff #ff0000
    Discharging at 21W
    48 minutes remaining

.fi
draws '17%' in white on red, with the detail there for whoever hovers over it.
An item needs no click action to have a tooltip.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following entry denotes a simple button for launching xterm, on the left
side of the panel.
.nf

    name=XTerm
    click=exec /usr/local/bin/xterm

.fi
A clock on the right-hand side, which updates itself every second:
.nf

    name=exec date +"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S"
    position=right
    updatesecs=1

.fi
If I have a 'wifi' script which can list available wifi networks, show the
current status, and switch wifi networks, I could expose it like this:
.nf

    name=exec wifi status
    updatesecs=10
    position=right
    menuitems=exec wifi list_networks
    menuclick=exec wifi pick_network "<item>"

.fi
Simple toggle menu to switch between front and back headphone sockets:
.nf

    name=exec $HOME/bin/audio-out status
    position=right
    updatesecs=1
    menuitems=front,back
    menuclick=exec $HOME/bin/audio-out <item>

.fi
Pure status display, which runs a script to get the current battery status
every 30s, but has no click action:
.nf

    name=exec $HOME/bin/battery-status
    position=right
    updatesecs=30

.fi
That script can colour itself and explain itself at the same time, by printing
a colour line and then some detail; see \fBTHE NAME PROTOCOL\fP.
Hopefully the above examples should give a reasonable indication as to what's
possible.


.SH "MULTIPLE MONITORS"
\fIGummiband\fP sits along the top of the topmost row of monitors, rather than
across the whole width of the display, so a monitor which is lower down or off
to one side is left alone.
.PP
When a window goes full screen on a monitor the panel is on, the panel moves
out of the way onto another monitor, and moves back when that window goes.
The monitor it moves to is the topmost one that nothing is filling, with the
leftmost winning a tie; if every monitor is covered, or there is only one, the
panel stays where it is.
.PP
Two kinds of window count as full screen. One is a window which asks for
\fB_NET_WM_STATE_FULLSCREEN\fP, which is what a toolkit does. The other is a
window simply sized and placed to cover a monitor exactly, which is what the
\&'borderless fullscreen' setting in a game's display options produces, and
what games run under Proton generally do. A merely maximised window is neither, and the
panel ignores it.
.PP
While the panel is away from the top of the display it reserves no space for
itself: \fB_NET_WM_STRUT\fP can only name an edge of the whole display, so a
top strut would reserve a strip across the very window the panel just got out
of the way of. It reserves its height again on returning home.

.SH RESOURCES
\fIGummiband\fP understands the following X resources:
.TP 14
.B font
name of the font used (default: 'roboto-16')
.TP 14
.B background
window background colour (default: 'white')
.TP 14
.B foreground
window foreground colour (default: 'black')
.TP 14
.B selBackground
background colour of currently-pointed-at actionable button (default: pale blue)
.TP 14
.B selForeground
foreground colour of currently-pointed-at actionable button (default: black)

.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
